lesson "Solving Trig Equations" {
  course: "pure-maths"
  unit: "Trigonometry"
  difficulty: "Advanced"
  icon: "X"
  summary: "Your calculator gives you one answer. There are always infinitely many, and exams want the ones in a stated range. How to find every last one of them."
  skills: ["trig-solving", "trig-all-solutions", "trig-quadrant-signs"]

  slide "The Calculator Lies by Omission" {
    id: "t4-hook"
    cat: "More Than One Answer"
    > Solve $\sin x = 0.5$.
    >
    > Your calculator says $30°$, and it is not wrong. It is just deeply incomplete.
    > $150°$ works too. So do $390°$, $510°$, and infinitely many more in both
    > directions.
    >
    > That is because $\sin x = 0.5$ is asking **where does the wave reach a height
    > of $0.5$**, and a wave that repeats forever crosses that height forever.
    >
    > Every orange dot below is a solution. Drag the slider and watch them slide
    > around, and notice that they never run out.

    scene plane {
      x: [-390, 780]
      y: [-1.7, 1.7]
      grid
      axes
      param k = 0.5 { range: [-1.4, 1.4], step: 0.05 }
      curve wave = sin(rad(x)) { color: primary, width: 3 }
      curve level = k { color: danger, width: 2, style: dashed }
      repeat n in range(0, 4) {
        point lo = (deg(asin(k)) + 360*(n-1), k) { color: accent, r: 7 }
        point hi = (180 - deg(asin(k)) + 360*(n-1), k) { color: accent, r: 7 }
      }
      label at (-370, 1.45) = "sin x = ${k}" { color: danger }
      slider k { label: "the value on the right-hand side" }
    }

    goal "Lift the line clear of the wave, until there are no solutions left at all" {
      when: abs(k) > 1.02
      hint: "The wave is trapped between -1 and 1. Take the line outside that and every dot has nowhere to land."
    }
  }

  slide "Two Per Turn" {
    id: "t4-two"
    cat: "More Than One Answer"
    > Within one full turn, $0°$ to $360°$, a sine equation normally has **two**
    > solutions, and the unit circle tells you exactly where the second one hides.
    >
    > Sine is the height. Two points on the circle sit at the same height: one in the
    > first quadrant and its mirror image in the second. So if $x_1$ is the
    > calculator's answer, the partner is
    >
    > $$x_2 = 180° - x_1$$
    >
    > For cosine, which is the across, the two matching points are mirrored in the
    > x-axis instead, so the partner is
    >
    > $$x_2 = 360° - x_1$$
    >
    > Tangent repeats twice as fast, every $180°$, so its partner is $x_1 + 180°$.

    scene plane {
      x: [-1.8, 1.8]
      y: [-1.5, 1.5]
      aspect: equal
      grid
      axes
      circle unit = (0, 0) { r: 1, color: neutral, opacity: 0 }
      line level = (-1.4, 0.5) -> (1.4, 0.5) { color: danger, style: dashed, width: 2 }
      point p1 = (cos(rad(30)), 0.5) { color: primary, r: 8, label: "30°" }
      point p2 = (cos(rad(150)), 0.5) { color: accent, r: 8, label: "150°" }
      line r1 = (0, 0) -> (cos(rad(30)), sin(rad(30))) { color: primary, width: 2 }
      line r2 = (0, 0) -> (cos(rad(150)), sin(rad(150))) { color: accent, width: 2 }
      label at (0, 1.32) = "same height, so the same sine" { anchor: middle, color: danger }
    }

    numeric {
      ask "One solution of $\sin x = 0.5$ is $30°$. What is the other solution between $0°$ and $360°$?"
      skill: "trig-all-solutions"
      answer: 150
      expect: 180 - 30
      tolerance: 0.5
      hint "For sine, the partner is $180°$ minus the first one."
      onwrong: "t4-find-the-partner" retry
      ! "$180° - 30° = 150°$. Both sit at height $0.5$, one on each side of the vertical."
    }
  }

  slide "Finding the Partner" {
    id: "t4-find-the-partner"
    hidden: true
    cat: "More Than One Answer"
    > The partner rule is worth deriving rather than memorising, because then you can
    > never mix up which is which.
    >
    > **Sine is the height.** Draw a horizontal line across the circle and it cuts in
    > two places, mirrored **left-to-right**. Reflecting an angle in the vertical axis
    > sends $\theta$ to $180° - \theta$. Hence the rule.
    >
    > **Cosine is the across.** Draw a vertical line and it cuts in two places,
    > mirrored **top-to-bottom**. Reflecting in the horizontal axis sends $\theta$ to
    > $360° - \theta$, or equivalently $-\theta$.
    >
    > So the rule follows from which coordinate you are fixing. Fix the height, mirror
    > sideways. Fix the across, mirror vertically.

    scene plane {
      x: [-4.2, 4.2]
      y: [-1.7, 1.9]
      aspect: equal
      axes: false
      circle cL = (-2.1, 0) { r: 1, color: neutral, opacity: 0 }
      circle cR = (2.1, 0) { r: 1, color: neutral, opacity: 0 }
      line hL = (-3.4, 0.5) -> (-0.8, 0.5) { color: danger, style: dashed, width: 2 }
      line vR = (2.1 + 0.5, -1.3) -> (2.1 + 0.5, 1.3) { color: success, style: dashed, width: 2 }
      point sa = (-2.1 + cos(rad(30)), 0.5) { color: primary, r: 7 }
      point sb = (-2.1 + cos(rad(150)), 0.5) { color: accent, r: 7 }
      point ca = (2.1 + 0.5, sin(rad(60))) { color: primary, r: 7 }
      point cb = (2.1 + 0.5, 0 - sin(rad(60))) { color: accent, r: 7 }
      label at (-2.1, 1.6) = "sine: mirror sideways" { anchor: middle, color: danger }
      label at (2.1, 1.6) = "cosine: mirror vertically" { anchor: middle, color: success }
      label at (-2.1, -1.5) = "180° - x" { anchor: middle, color: neutral }
      label at (2.1, -1.5) = "360° - x" { anchor: middle, color: neutral }
    }

    numeric {
      ask "One solution of $\cos x = 0.5$ is $60°$. What is the other one between $0°$ and $360°$?"
      skill: "trig-all-solutions"
      answer: 300
      expect: 360 - 60
      tolerance: 0.5
      hint "For cosine the partner is $360°$ minus the first."
      ! "$360° - 60° = 300°$. Both have the same across-value of $0.5$, one above the axis and one below."
    }
  }

  slide "The Method" {
    id: "t4-method"
    cat: "Working Through a Range"
    > A reliable routine for "solve for $0° \le x \le 360°$":
    >
    > **One.** Get the trig function alone on one side.
    >
    > **Two.** Take the inverse to get the calculator's answer, $x_1$.
    >
    > **Three.** Use the partner rule for that function to get $x_2$.
    >
    > **Four.** Add or subtract $360°$ (or $180°$ for tangent) to sweep out any
    > further solutions, and keep only the ones inside the stated range.
    >
    > Step four is the one people skip, and it is where most of the lost marks live.

    order {
      ask "Put the steps in order for solving $2\sin x - 1 = 0$ for $0° \le x \le 360°$."
      skill: "trig-solving"
      item "Rearrange to $\sin x = 0.5$"
      item "Take the inverse sine: $x_1 = 30°$"
      item "Apply the sine partner rule: $x_2 = 180° - 30° = 150°$"
      item "Check for more by adding $360°$: the next would be $390°$"
      item "Discard $390°$ as outside the range, leaving $30°$ and $150°$"
      decoy "Stop after the calculator gives $30°$"
      ! "Rearrange, invert, partner, sweep, filter. The decoy is exactly the habit this method exists to break."
    }
  }

  slide "A Negative Right-Hand Side" {
    id: "t4-negative"
    cat: "Working Through a Range"
    > When the value is negative the calculator gets awkward. $\sin^{-1}(-0.5)$
    > returns $-30°$, which is a perfectly good angle but not in $0°$ to $360°$.
    >
    > Do not fight it. Work with the **positive** version to find the reference angle,
    > then place your answers in the quadrants where sine is negative — the third and
    > fourth.
    >
    > $\sin 30° = 0.5$, so the reference angle is $30°$. Sine is negative below the
    > axis, giving
    >
    > $$x = 180° + 30° = 210° \qquad \text{and} \qquad x = 360° - 30° = 330°$$

    numeric {
      ask "Solve $\sin x = -0.5$ for $0° \le x \le 360°$ and give the **smaller** solution, in degrees."
      skill: "trig-all-solutions"
      answer: 210
      expect: 180 + 30
      tolerance: 0.5
      hint "Reference angle $30°$, and sine is negative in the third and fourth quadrants."
      ! "$210°$ and $330°$. Taking the calculator's $-30°$ and stopping would have scored nothing, even though $-30°$ genuinely solves the equation."
    }
  }

  slide "Watch the Multiplier" {
    id: "t4-multiple"
    cat: "Working Through a Range"
    > One last trap, and it is a good one. Solve $\sin 2x = 0.5$ for
    > $0° \le x \le 360°$.
    >
    > If $x$ runs over $0°$ to $360°$, then $2x$ runs over $0°$ to **$720°$** — two
    > full turns. So you must find all the solutions for $2x$ across that doubled
    > range, and only then halve them.
    >
    > $2x = 30°, 150°, 390°, 510°$, which gives $x = 15°, 75°, 195°, 255°$.
    >
    > **Four** solutions, not two. Solve for the angle inside the function first, over
    > its own stretched range, and halve at the very end.
    >
    > You do not have to take that on trust. Step the multiplier up and count the dots
    > for yourself.

    scene plane {
      x: [-20, 380]
      y: [-1.6, 1.6]
      grid
      axes
      param n = 2 { range: [1, 4], step: 1 }
      curve wave = sin(rad(n*x)) { color: primary, width: 3 }
      curve level = 0.5 { color: danger, width: 2, style: dashed }
      repeat i in range(0, 4) {
        point lo = (30/n + 360*i/n, 0.5) { color: accent, r: 7, show: 30/n + 360*i/n < 360 }
        point hi = (150/n + 360*i/n, 0.5) { color: accent, r: 7, show: 150/n + 360*i/n < 360 }
      }
      label at (10, 1.42) = "sin ${n}x = 0.5 has ${2*n} solutions between 0° and 360°" { color: primary }
      stepper n { label: "the multiplier" }
    }

    goal "Set the multiplier to 3 and count the dots" {
      when: n >= 3
      hint: "Each press squeezes another whole turn of the wave into the same stretch of x."
    }
  }

  slide "Count Them" {
    id: "t4-multiple-check"
    cat: "Working Through a Range"
    > The wave got squeezed, the range did not. That is the entire trick: a multiplier
    > of $n$ packs $n$ turns into the same $0°$ to $360°$, and every turn brings its
    > own pair of solutions.

    quiz {
      ask "How many solutions does $\sin 3x = 0.5$ have for $0° \le x \le 360°$?"
      skill: "trig-solving"
      * "Six"
      - "Two" { why: "That is how many $\sin\theta = 0.5$ has in one turn. But $3x$ sweeps through three turns while $x$ goes round once, so there are three times as many." }
      - "Three" { why: "The $3$ multiplies the number of turns, and each turn contributes two solutions, so it is $3 \times 2$." }
      hint "If $x$ goes from $0°$ to $360°$, what range does $3x$ cover?"
      ! "$3x$ runs over $0°$ to $1080°$, three full turns, and each turn holds two solutions. Six in total."
    }
  }

  slide "All Together" {
    id: "t4-synth"
    cat: "Working Through a Range"
    > Solve $\cos x = -0.5$ for $0° \le x \le 360°$.

    numeric {
      ask "Give the **larger** of the two solutions, in degrees."
      skill: "trig-all-solutions"
      answer: 240
      expect: 360 - 120
      tolerance: 0.5
      hint "Reference angle is $60°$, since $\cos 60° = 0.5$. Cosine is negative in the second and third quadrants."
      ! "$120°$ and $240°$. The reference angle $60°$ sets the size; the quadrants where cosine is negative decide where they sit."
    }
  }
}
